.TH iron-vault-config-check 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-config\-check \- Parse and validate FILE without contacting the service: no running service, vault or root is needed. It prints `Config ok` and exits 0, or prints why the config is not valid, with the line and column, and exits 1; it exits 2 when FILE cannot be read or is longer than the limit. It does not check what only an approval can: that each credential exists and permits its route\*(Aqs host, that a peer caller\*(Aqs account exists, or the upstream TLS and log destination settings. A config that passes can still be refused by `config approve`
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault config check\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIFILE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Parse and validate FILE without contacting the service: no running service, vault or root is needed. It prints `Config ok` and exits 0, or prints why the config is not valid, with the line and column, and exits 1; it exits 2 when FILE cannot be read or is longer than the limit. It does not check what only an approval can: that each credential exists and permits its route\*(Aqs host, that a peer caller\*(Aqs account exists, or the upstream TLS and log destination settings. A config that passes can still be refused by `config approve`
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIFILE\fR>
The config file to check
